\begin{abstract}
	Despite the remarkable creative power of modern Digital Audio Workstations (DAWs), a persistent \textit{semantic gap} exists between how musicians conceptually describe desired sounds (e.g., "warm," "aggressive," "spacious") and the technical parameters required to achieve them. Recent advances in generative AI enable direct text-to-audio synthesis, but produce finalized waveforms that cannot be edited within professional production workflows. We present \textbf{Audio-Agent}, a neuro-symbolic framework that bridges this gap by generating \textit{editable Digital Signal Processing (DSP) parameters} rather than waveforms, enabling intuitive control over high-fidelity audio processing chains.

	Our key innovations address fundamental limitations in semantic audio control: (1) \textbf{Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR)}, which captures audio texture through second-order statistical representations (Gram matrices) rather than simple mean pooling, improving style-consistent retrieval by 35\%; (2) \textbf{Dual-Modal Adaptive Fusion}, combining textual semantics and acoustic embeddings with uncertainty-aware dynamic weighting, demonstrating superior robustness to noisy references and missing modalities; and (3) \textbf{Constrained Parameter Generation}, formalizing Large Language Model (LLM) output within physically valid parameter spaces to reduce hallucination by 78\% while maintaining full editability.

	Comprehensive evaluation across 50 guitar tone samples demonstrates consistent improvements on objective metrics: parameter distance drops from 42.67 (zero-shot) to 21.86, Acc@0.1 rises from 0.12 to 0.45, cosine similarity improves from 0.45 to 0.78, and active-parameter recall increases from 0.15 to 0.49. We also prepare a MUSHRA-style interface for future subjective evaluation. Audio-Agent represents a practical system enabling natural language control of professional audio processing while preserving parametric transparency and creative flexibility.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\subsection{The Creativity--Complexity Paradox in Modern Audio Production}

Professional music production faces a fundamental paradox. On one hand, modern Digital Audio Workstations (DAWs) provide unprecedented creative control---dozens of parameters per effects chain, each precisely adjustable to 0.01\,dB resolution. On the other hand, this very abundance has created a \textbf{cognitive barrier} that disproportionately impacts emerging artists: the gap between \textit{perceptual intent} (e.g., ``warm, punchy, aggressive'') and \textit{technical execution} (e.g., threshold=$-18.5$\,dB, ratio=$4.3{:}1$, attack=$12.3$\,ms) has widened rather than narrowed. This is the \textit{Semantic Gap} \citep{stables2014semantic} in its most practical form.

Consider a concrete scenario: an independent guitarist wants to achieve a ``vintage breakup'' tone reminiscent of classic British rock. Without expensive trial-and-error or professional assistance, she confronts three interrelated obstacles. First, a typical amplifier and effects chain involves 8--15 continuous parameters with non-linear interactions, creating a combinatorial explosion of possible configurations. Second, semantic descriptors such as ``breakup'' admit multiple valid interpretations across genres, ranging from smooth overdrive to harsh clipping or compressed saturation, making it difficult to translate perceptual intent into precise technical specifications. Third, presets exhibit limited transferability; a configuration that works for one guitar--pedal--amp combination often fails when applied to different equipment, necessitating time-consuming recalibration.

This is not merely a usability issue---it is a \textbf{creativity bottleneck}. Informal workflow studies and practitioner reports suggest that up to 40\% of session time can be spent on technical parameter tweaking rather than creative decision-making. For democratizing music production, bridging this gap is as important as making professional tooling accessible in the first place.

\subsection{The Challenge of Parameter Intuition}
Bridging this gap has been a long-standing goal of Intelligent Music Production (IMP). Traditional approaches often rely on audio content analysis to automate mixing tasks, such as fader balancing or EQ matching. While effective for technical corrections, these systems often lack the flexibility to handle creative, style-driven timbre design.

Recent advancements in generative AI, such as AudioLDM \citep{liu2023audioldm} and MusicLM \citep{agostinelli2023musiclm}, have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in text-to-audio synthesis. However, these ``black-box'' models typically generate raw waveforms directly. This poses a critical limitation for professional workflows: the output is finalized and non-editable. A producer cannot tweak the ``attack time'' of a generated snare nor adjust the ``drive'' of a synthesized guitar riff. Consequently, despite their novelty, these tools remain isolated from the standard parametric workflow of modern production.

\subsection{Neuro-Symbolic Control and Hallucination}
To combine the semantic understanding of Large Language Models (LLMs) with the precision of classic DSP, a \textit{Neuro-Symbolic} approach—where an LLM acts as an agent to control symbolic DSP parameters—emerges as a promising solution. However, applying naive LLMs to this task reveals a ``hallucination'' problem in the parameter space. Without grounding, an LLM might infer plausible-looking but sonically incoherent settings (e.g., a compressor with a Ratio of 1:1 and a Threshold of 0dB, effectively doing nothing, despite a prompt asking for ``heavy compression''). Furthermore, generic LLMs lack specific knowledge of user-defined presets or niche musical sub-genres (e.g., ``Shoegaze'' or ``Djent'').

To quantify the severity of this issue, Table \ref{tab:hallucination} reports representative constraint-violation rates observed in our zero-shot baseline, where a \textit{violation} is defined as any generated parameter value falling outside the plugin-defined valid range.

\begin{table}[t]
	\centering
	\caption{Parameter range violations in zero-shot LLM generation. A violation occurs when a generated value falls outside the plugin-defined valid range. Results based on 50 test queries across three common parameter types.}
	\label{tab:hallucination}
	\small
	\begin{tabular}{lccc}
		\csname toprule\endcsname
		\csname textbf\endcsname{Parameter Type} & \csname textbf\endcsname{Valid Range} & \csname textbf\endcsname{LLM Output} & \csname textbf\endcsname{Violation Rate} \\
		\midrule
		Threshold (dB)                           & [$-60$, $0$]                          & $-5.2$, $-3.1$, \textbf{$+8.7$}      & 12.3\%                                   \\
		Ratio                                    & [$1.0$, $20.0$]                       & $4.3$, $2.1$, \textbf{$0.8$}         & 18.7\%                                   \\
		Frequency (Hz)                           & [$20$, $20000$]                       & $1200$, \textbf{$50000$}, $800$      & 8.2\%                                    \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We identify three root causes that make unconstrained, zero-shot parameter generation brittle in practice. First, the lack of grounding in concrete, previously validated presets leads to unguided exploration of the parameter space. This allows the model to generate plausible-looking values that have never been auditioned in real production contexts. Second, sparse and biased training signals for niche sub-genres and production practices limit generalization to specialized use cases. Generic LLMs are not trained on detailed audio engineering workflows, making them unreliable for domain-specific parameter inference. Third, non-linear parameter interactions cause local edits to behave unintuitively. This makes it difficult to predict the perceptual effect of individual parameter adjustments without understanding the full interaction graph of the DSP chain.

In this paper, we address these challenges with \textbf{Audio-Agent}, a neuro-symbolic system that generates editable DSP parameters from natural language descriptions. Our key contributions span theoretical, algorithmic, evaluation, and system-level advances.

From a \textbf{theoretical perspective}, we propose Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR), a second-order statistical representation for audio texture that captures timbral structure through Gram matrix embeddings. TRR improves style-consistent retrieval by 35\% over mean-pooled baselines and demonstrates clear advantages on texture-sensitive cases (Table \ref{tab:exp4}).

On the \textbf{algorithmic front}, we introduce a dual-modal adaptive fusion strategy that combines textual semantics and acoustic evidence with uncertainty-aware weighting. This yields a 42\% reduction in error under degraded prompts compared to text-only retrieval (Table \ref{tab:robustness}).

Our \textbf{evaluation contribution} provides a multi-dimensional objective evaluation suite including L2 distance, Acc@0.1, cosine similarity, and active-parameter recall. We report comprehensive results across 50 guitar tone samples (Table \ref{tab:main_results}).

Finally, from a \textbf{system perspective}, we implement a practical JUCE-based DSP client integrated with a Python retrieval-and-reasoning agent. This delivers an extensible framework that enables editable parameter outputs compatible with standard production workflows.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:related_work} reviews related work in IMP and neural audio. Section \ref{sec:problem_formulation} formalizes the parameter inference problem. Section \ref{sec:methodology} details the Audio-Agent architecture and algorithms. Section \ref{sec:experiments} presents our experimental evaluation, and Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

Our work lies at the intersection of intelligent music production, neural audio synthesis, and LLM-based agent systems. We review each area and position our contributions accordingly.

\subsection{Intelligent Music Production}

The challenge of translating perceptual descriptions into technical parameters was formally characterized by \citet{stables2014semantic}. Their SAFE project demonstrated the feasibility of semantic-to-parameter mapping in a DAW context, but \textbf{did not resolve} the generalization problem across instruments, effect chains, and stylistic sub-genres. \citet{schedl2014survey} provides a comprehensive survey of music information retrieval techniques, highlighting semantic auto-tagging as a key challenge.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Audio Analysis Approaches}. Subsequent work pursued automation via audio content analysis and differentiable mixing systems \citep{martinez2020deep,steinmetz2022automix,moliner2025megami}. While effective when a target mix or reference audio already exists, this direction \textbf{implicitly assumes the target audio is given}. It therefore struggles to support creative timbre design from a purely conceptual request (``make it more vintage breakup'') and often suffers from an \textbf{opacity problem}: users see outputs but lack interpretable, editable rationale.

Recent advances in differentiable DSP have enabled neural approaches to audio effect modeling. \citet{peladeau2024blind} proposes blind estimation of audio effect parameters using auto-encoder architectures, addressing the inverse problem of parameter recovery. \citet{liu2024ddspSFX} introduces DDSP-SFX, which leverages differentiable digital signal processing for acoustically-guided sound effects generation, combining neural networks with traditional DSP modules for controllable synthesis. \citet{deemanat2025latent} explores generative latent spaces for neural audio synthesis, bridging DSP-informed and data-driven approaches. In parallel, style transfer methods have been applied to audio effects, with \citet{benetos2024stito} introducing ST-ITO for controlling audio effects via inference-time optimization using pretrained audio representations.

\csname textbf\endcsname{The LLM Frontier}. Recent work explores LLMs as natural language interfaces for effect parameters \citep{doh2025llm2fx,doh2025llm2fxtools,clemens2025mixassist}. While demonstrating surprising capability, these approaches remain vulnerable to hallucination: reported violation rates can reach \textbf{up to 35\%} in zero-shot settings, producing invalid or incoherent parameters. Dialogue-based disambiguation can reduce ambiguity but shifts the burden to the user and does not scale to complex multi-effect chains.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Our Positioning}. Unlike prior work that treats parameter prediction as a \textit{regression problem} or a purely generative problem, we frame it as a \textit{retrieval-grounded reasoning problem}: retrieval supplies concrete, previously validated exemplars, and generation becomes an adaptation step rather than invention.

\subsection{Neural Audio Synthesis}

Neural audio synthesis has advanced rapidly, enabling high-fidelity text-to-audio generation \citep{liu2023audioldm,liu2024audioldm2,agostinelli2023musiclm,vyas2023audiobox}. Diffusion models have emerged as the dominant paradigm, with \citet{huang2022makeanaudio} introducing prompt-enhanced text-to-audio generation, \citet{wang2023audiolcm} achieving efficient high-quality synthesis via latent consistency models, and \citet{liang2024instruction} exploring instruction-guided latent diffusion for better controllability. However, these models primarily output \textit{finalized waveforms}, which are difficult to edit within professional workflows.

Accordingly, we view parameter-level generation as a \textbf{complement to, not a replacement for}, waveform-level synthesis: it targets controllable editing and iterative refinement inside DAWs.

This motivates what we call \textbf{Parameter--Waveform Duality}: waveform-level generation offers maximal expressivity but limited editability, whereas parameter-level control preserves transparency and iterative refinement at the cost of requiring domain knowledge.

\begin{table}[t]
	\centering
	\caption{Comparison of waveform-level and parameter-level approaches in neural audio workflows. Each paradigm offers complementary strengths for different production contexts.}
	\label{tab:param_waveform_duality}
	\small
	\begin{tabular}{lcc}
		\csname toprule\endcsname
		\csname textbf\endcsname{Level} & \csname textbf\endcsname{Primary Strength} & \csname textbf\endcsname{Primary Limitation} \\
		\midrule
		Waveform-level                  & High-fidelity synthesis                    & Hard to edit in DAWs                         \\
		Parameter-level                 & Fully editable control                     & Requires expert priors                       \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

In parallel, differentiable DSP (DDSP) \citep{engel2020ddsp} demonstrates that learned models can control interpretable synthesis parameters, and self-supervised audio representation learning (e.g., Wav2Vec 2.0 \citep{baevski2020wav2vec}) provides strong acoustic embeddings. Audio-Agent leverages these representations for similarity grounding, but targets editable parameter outputs compatible with DAW-centric workflows.

\subsection{LLM Agents and Retrieval-Augmented Generation}

Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) \citep{lewis2020rag} improves reliability by grounding generation in external memory. Multimodal variants extend this idea beyond text \citep{yasunaga2023retrieval,liu2025hmrag}, with recent developments including MMKB-RAG \citep{zayats2024mmkb} for multi-modal knowledge-based systems, and frameworks for visually-rich document understanding \citep{jin2024mrag}. Instruction manual understanding approaches \citep{jin2024multimodal} demonstrate the value of cross-modal grounding in practical applications. Agentic tool-use frameworks (e.g., \citep{schick2023toolformer,yao2023react,sun2024toolgen}) enable language models to act through structured interfaces.

However, applying RAG to audio parameter generation reveals an \textbf{Audio Modality Gap}. Text-only retrieval struggles with semantic ambiguity (``warm'' and ``breakup'' vary by genre), while audio-only retrieval can over-emphasize perceptual similarity without capturing \textit{functional similarity} in parameter space. Audio-Agent addresses this gap with \textbf{dual-modal grounding} and an \textbf{uncertainty-aware} fusion mechanism tailored to parameter inference.

This issue persists even when using strong general-purpose audio representations (e.g., CLAP- or PaSST-style embeddings): perceptual similarity does not necessarily imply that the underlying effect parameters are transferable. Recent audio-language models such as CLAP \citep{elizalde2023clap}, ReCLAP \citep{chen2024reclap}, and M2D-CLAP \citep{niizumi2024m2dclap} have improved zero-shot audio classification through better text-audio alignment, while few-shot prompt learning techniques \citep{wu2024fewshot} enable better adaptation to downstream tasks. However, these models primarily target classification and captioning rather than parameter estimation.

\subsection{Audio Texture and Semantic Audio Retrieval}

The concept of audio texture has been studied extensively in the context of style transfer and synthesis. \citet{gatys2015style} introduced Gram matrix-based style representations for images, which were later adapted to audio by \citet{stylianou2015audio} and others. Recent surveys on audio texture analysis \citep{mcwec2023texture} and audio representations for sound synthesis \citep{verzotto2022representations} highlight the importance of capturing second-order statistical features for timbre characterization.

In semantic audio retrieval, the WikiMuTe dataset \citep{weck2023wikimute} provides a rich resource for connecting natural language descriptions with music audio, while retrieval-guided approaches \citep{srivatsan2024retrieval} demonstrate the value of combining retrieval with generation for music captioning. Retrieval-augmented text-to-music generation \citep{gonzales2024rag} further validates the effectiveness of RAG approaches in audio tasks.

Our Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR) builds upon these insights by using Gram matrices to capture audio texture through second-order statistics, enabling more style-consistent retrieval for parameter inference.

