\section{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

Let $f(\mathbf{x}, \theta)$ denote the audio effect chain, where $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^T$ is the input audio signal of length $T$, and $\theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ represents the $d$-dimensional parameter vector constrained to a valid parameter space $\Theta$. The user's intent is expressed as $I = \{T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}}\}$, where $T_{\text{desc}}$ is a natural language description (e.g., ``warm vintage compression'') and $A_{\text{ref}}$ is an optional reference audio sample. If the reference audio is absent, the system falls back to text-only retrieval and reasoning.

The objective is to learn a mapping $\mathcal{M}: I \rightarrow \theta^*$ such that:
\begin{equation}
	\theta^* = \arg\min_{\theta \in \Theta} \mathcal{L}_{\text{semantic}}(f(\mathbf{x}, \theta), I) + \lambda \mathcal{L}_{\text{preference}}(\theta, \mathcal{H})
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_{\text{semantic}}$ measures semantic alignment between the processed audio and user intent, $\mathcal{L}_{\text{preference}}$ captures alignment with historical user preferences $\mathcal{H}$, and $\lambda$ balances these objectives.

In practice, these objectives are approximated by retrieval-guided parameter inference and evaluated using operational metrics such as parameter distance, tolerance accuracy, and direction consistency. Direct optimization is intractable due to the non-differentiable nature of semantic similarity and the high dimensionality of the parameter space. We therefore decompose the problem into a \textbf{retrieval} step and a \textbf{reasoning} step:
\begin{equation}
	\theta^* = \text{LLM}\left(T_{\text{desc}}, \text{RAG}(T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}}), \mathcal{H}\right)
\end{equation}
This decomposition motivates three concrete challenges addressed in our method: retrieving texture-consistent references, fusing heterogeneous modalities, and constraining parameter outputs to remain usable in practice.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

Our methodology consists of a hybrid neuro-symbolic architecture that bridges semantic user intent with precise DSP parameter control. We formalize the problem and detail the system design, retrieval mechanisms, and adaptive learning algorithms.

\subsection{System Architecture}
\label{ssec:architecture}

Audio-Agent adopts a hybrid neuro-symbolic architecture with three layers: (1) a \textbf{C++ Client} for real-time DSP processing, (2) a \textbf{Bridging Layer} for inter-process communication, and (3) a \textbf{Python Agent} for intelligent retrieval and reasoning.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/system_architecture.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure 1: System architecture showing the C++ DSP client, Python RAG agent, and bidirectional communication flow - to be included]}
	\caption{System architecture showing the C++ DSP client, Python RAG agent, and bidirectional communication flow.}
	\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{C++ Client (JUCE DSP Engine)}

The C++ client handles real-time audio processing requirements. The JUCE-based DSP engine operates with low latency; exact buffer and sample-rate settings are implementation details rather than methodological assumptions.

\textbf{Real-time Audio Processing Pipeline}:
\begin{align}
	\mathbf{x}_{\text{out}}[n]            & = \text{DSP}_{\text{chain}}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{in}}[n], \theta_{\text{current}}) \\
	\text{where } \theta_{\text{current}} & = \text{Interpolate}(\theta_{\text{previous}}, \theta_{\text{target}}, \alpha)
\end{align}
where $\alpha \in [0, 1]$ is a smoothing factor for parameter transitions.

\textbf{Parameter Update Mechanism}: We use a double-buffered approach with a ring buffer to decouple real-time processing from parameter updates. The buffer holds up to 100 parameter sets, allowing smooth parameter changes without audio artifacts.

\textbf{Thread Safety}: The DSP engine runs on a high-priority real-time thread (REALTIME\_PRIORITY), while parameter updates occur on a separate background thread. Communication uses lock-free queues implemented with JUCE's \texttt{AbstractFifo} class.

\textbf{WebSocket Bridge}: The client exposes a WebSocket server (port 8765) that accepts JSON-formatted parameter messages with the schema:
\begin{equation}
	\text{Message} = \{\theta: \mathbb{R}^d, \text{timestamp}: \mathbb{R}, \text{metadata}: \mathcal{M}\}
\end{equation}

The bridge layer handles message serialization, deserialization, and validation. Invalid parameter sets are rejected with error codes, and the system falls back to the last valid parameter configuration.

\subsubsection{Python Agent (RAG and Reasoning Engine)}

The Python agent manages the intelligent components: (a) knowledge base construction and indexing, (b) dual-modal retrieval, (c) LLM-based reasoning, and (d) adaptive preference learning.

\textbf{Knowledge Base Architecture}: We maintain two complementary knowledge bases. The first, $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}$, stores text descriptions and style tags that support semantic retrieval, while the second, $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$, contains audio vectors and texture-resonance features that capture timbral structure.

In our experiments, these are instantiated as a lightweight retrieval store populated from the parameter and audio-vector databases, and the retrieval backend is treated as a swappable component.

\textbf{LLM Inference}: The reasoning module is treated as a pluggable generator. In our experiments, we use a lightweight generation agent and focus on how retrieval context influences parameter inference, rather than on a specific model implementation.

