\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Communication Protocol}

The bidirectional communication between the Python agent and C++ client follows a WebSocket-based protocol with JSON message format.

\paragraph{Parameter Update Message.}
When the Python agent generates new parameters, it sends a message with the following schema:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "type": "parameter_update",
  "timestamp": 1234567890.123,
  "parameters": {
    "eq_low_gain": 2.5,
    "eq_mid_freq": 1200.0,
    "comp_threshold": -18.5,
    "comp_ratio": 4.3,
    ...
  },
  "metadata": {
    "query": "warm vintage breakup",
    "retrieved_presets": ["Tweed Clean", "British Crunch"],
    "confidence": 0.87
  }
}
\end{verbatim}

\paragraph{Acknowledgment and Error Handling.}
The C++ client responds with an acknowledgment message indicating success or failure:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "type": "ack",
  "status": "success" | "error",
  "message": "Parameters applied successfully" | "Invalid range for comp_ratio",
  "applied_parameters": {...}
}
\end{verbatim}

If parameter validation fails on the C++ side (e.g., due to plugin-specific constraints not captured in the Python agent), the client rejects the update and maintains the previous valid state. This provides a safety net against edge cases in constraint specification.

\paragraph{Latency and Throughput.}
The WebSocket protocol introduces minimal latency (typically < 10ms on localhost). Parameter updates are asynchronous and do not block audio processing. The system can handle up to 100 parameter updates per second, though typical usage involves 1-2 updates per user query.

\subsection{Dual-Modal Retrieval-Augmented Generation}
\label{ssec:rag}

Our RAG system grounds LLM inference in concrete, retrieved examples from both textual and audio modalities. This dual-modal approach mitigates hallucinations while providing rich contextual information for parameter reasoning.

\subsubsection{Knowledge Base Construction}

We first formalize the construction of our dual knowledge bases:

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Text Knowledge Base Construction}
	\label{alg:text_kb}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Style descriptions $\mathcal{S}$, parameter templates $\mathcal{P}$
		\ENSURE Text knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}$

		\STATE Initialize empty knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}$
		\FORALL{style $s$}
		\STATE Parse textual description: $\text{Tags}(s) = \{g, t, e\}$
		\COMMENT{where $g$=genre, $t$=texture, $e$=effect}
		\STATE Retrieve relevant templates: $\mathcal{P}_s = \text{Retrieve}(s)$
		\STATE Build semantic graph: $\mathcal{G}_s = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})$
		\STATE Encode to embeddings: $\mathbf{e}_{\text{text}} = \text{SBERT}(s)$
		\STATE Store: $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}[s] = (\text{Tags}(s), \mathcal{P}_s, \mathcal{G}_s, \mathbf{e}_{\text{text}})$
		\ENDFOR
		\RETURN $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{Why Second-Order Statistics?}

Most audio retrieval pipelines compress frame-level representations into a single embedding via mean pooling. This implicitly assumes that first-order statistics are sufficient: if two signals share a similar average embedding, they are treated as similar. While effective for coarse semantic similarity, this assumption can fail for \textit{timbral texture}.

For a toy example, two tones can achieve a high cosine similarity between mean-pooled embeddings (e.g., $\cos(\bar{\mathbf{h}}_1,\bar{\mathbf{h}}_2) \approx 0.95$) while remaining perceptually distinct, because the temporal structure of co-activations is discarded.

\paragraph{A Concrete Contrast.} Consider two guitar tones:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Sustained distortion}: a heavily overdriven tone with near-constant harmonic content.
	\item \textbf{Modulated chorus}: a clean tone with slow LFO modulation, producing periodic spectral variation.
\end{enumerate}
Both can exhibit similar mean-pooled embeddings (e.g., comparable average spectral centroid), yet they are perceptually and functionally distinct. Mean pooling struggles because it discards the \textit{temporal structure} of feature co-activations that differentiates non-linear saturation from time-varying modulation.

\subsubsection{The Texture Hypothesis}

We hypothesize that audio \textit{timbre} is better characterized by \textit{feature relationships} than by individual feature magnitudes. This is analogous to visual style transfer, where style is captured by feature correlations rather than pixel-level content \citep{gatys2015style}. In our setting, correlations between deep acoustic channels encode how spectral and temporal patterns co-occur (e.g., ``brightness'' with ``roughness''), which directly relates to which classes of effects are active (distortion, modulation, dynamics, and filtering). This framing is also consistent with findings in auditory neuroscience: timbre perception depends on distributed population responses and interactions, not solely on marginal feature magnitudes.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Audio Knowledge Base Construction}
	\label{alg:audio_kb}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Parameter presets $\mathcal{P}$, audio samples $\mathcal{A}$
		\ENSURE Audio knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$

		\STATE Initialize empty knowledge base $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$
		\FORALL{preset $p$}
		\STATE Extract audio features: $\mathbf{h} = \text{Wav2Vec2}(\text{Audio}(p))$
		\STATE Compute texture resonance: $\mathbf{G} = \text{GramMatrix}(\mathbf{h})$
		\STATE Validate parameter space: $\theta_{\text{valid}} = \text{ValidateConstraints}(p)$
		\STATE Construct embedding: $\mathbf{e}_{\text{audio}} = \text{Concat}(\mathbf{h}, \mathbf{G}, p)$
		\STATE Store with metadata: $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}[p] = (\mathbf{e}_{\text{audio}}, \theta_{\text{valid}}, \text{Meta}(p))$
		\ENDFOR
		\RETURN $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsubsection{Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR)}

We introduce TRR as a novel second-order statistical representation for audio texture that addresses fundamental limitations of mean-pooled embeddings in distinguishing non-stationary textures. Given an audio reference $a_{\text{ref}}$, we extract intermediate representations $\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T}$ from the 9th layer of Wav2Vec2 (768-dim, pre-trained on LibriSpeech). The audio is preprocessed at 16kHz sample rate with a 25ms window and 10ms hop length.

The texture resonance is computed as the Gram matrix:
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{G} = \frac{1}{T} \mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times C}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T}$ are the frame-level feature activations with $C$ channels and $T$ time steps, and $\mathbf{G}_{ij}$ captures the correlation between channels $i$ and $j$ across time, encoding how different acoustic features co-activate.

To ensure scale invariance and numerical stability, we apply spectral normalization:
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}} = \frac{\mathbf{G}}{\|\mathbf{G}\|_F}
\end{equation}
where $\|\mathbf{G}\|_F$ is the Frobenius norm, normalizing for differences in overall signal energy.

The similarity between query and database textures is computed via cosine similarity on the normalized Gram matrices:
\begin{equation}
	\text{sim}_{\text{TRR}}(\mathbf{G}_q, \mathbf{G}_d) = \frac{\text{tr}(\mathbf{G}_q \mathbf{G}_d^\top)}{\|\mathbf{G}_q\|_F \|\mathbf{G}_d\|_F}
\end{equation}

\csname textbf\endcsname{Invariances}. By aggregating across time, TRR is approximately time-shift invariant: it preserves \textit{what relationships occur}, not \textit{when} they occur. The normalization step provides scale invariance to global energy.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Connection to Parameter Space}. Many effect families manifest primarily as changes in channel co-activation structure rather than in the mean feature magnitude (e.g., saturation introduces correlated harmonics; chorus creates periodic correlations). TRR therefore acts as a \textit{texture prior} for retrieving parameter presets that are more likely to activate the appropriate effect subset.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Intuition}. While mean-pooled embeddings capture first-order statistics (what textures are present), Gram matrices capture second-order statistics (how textures relate to each other), which is crucial for distinguishing effects like distortion (harmonic relationships) and modulation (temporal correlations).

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Complete Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR) Algorithm}
	\label{alg:trr_complete}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Audio sample $a$, sample rate $sr=16000$, Wav2Vec2 model $\mathcal{W}$
		\ENSURE TRR embedding $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times C}$ where $C=768$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Audio Preprocessing}
		\STATE Resample $a$ to 16kHz if needed
		\STATE Normalize amplitude: $a \leftarrow a / \max(|a|)$
		\STATE Ensure minimum length: $T_{\text{audio}} \geq 1$ second

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: Feature Extraction}
		\STATE Extract frame-level features from layer 9 of Wav2Vec2:
		\STATE $\mathbf{H} \leftarrow \mathcal{W}_{\text{layer 9}}(a) \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T}$
		\STATE \textit{where $C=768$ is feature dimension, $T$ is number of time frames}

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Gram Matrix Computation}
		\STATE Compute second-order statistics:
		\STATE $\mathbf{G} \leftarrow \frac{1}{T} \mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times C}$
		\STATE \textit{$\mathbf{G}_{ij}$ captures correlation between channels $i$ and $j$}

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 4: Spectral Normalization}
		\STATE Compute Frobenius norm: $\sigma \leftarrow \|\mathbf{G}\|_F = \sqrt{\sum_{i,j} \mathbf{G}_{ij}^2}$
		\STATE Normalize for scale invariance: $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}} \leftarrow \mathbf{G} / \sigma$

		\RETURN $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}}$

		\STATE
		\STATE \textbf{Complexity Analysis:}
		\STATE \textit{Time}: $\mathcal{O}(T \cdot C)$ for feature extraction, $\mathcal{O}(C^2 \cdot T)$ for Gram matrix
		\STATE \textit{Space}: $\mathcal{O}(C \cdot T)$ for features, $\mathcal{O}(C^2)$ for Gram matrix
		\STATE \textit{Total}: $\mathcal{O}(C^2 \cdot T)$ time, $\mathcal{O}(C^2)$ space when features are not cached
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/trr_feature_extraction.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: TRR Feature Extraction Pipeline. This figure should illustrate the complete process: (1) Input audio waveform at 16kHz, (2) Wav2Vec2 feature extraction from layer 9 producing $\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{768 \times T}$, (3) Gram matrix computation $\mathbf{G} = \frac{1}{T}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top$, (4) Spectral normalization to produce $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}}$. Show data dimensions at each stage and use arrows to indicate flow. Include a small heatmap visualization of an example Gram matrix to illustrate the second-order structure.]}
	\caption{Texture Resonance feature extraction pipeline. The process transforms audio input through Wav2Vec2 feature extraction, Gram matrix computation to capture second-order statistics, and spectral normalization for scale invariance.}
	\label{fig:trr_pipeline}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/gram_matrix_computation.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Gram Matrix Computation Visualization. This figure should show: (1) Frame-level feature matrix $\mathbf{H}$ as a 2D heatmap ($C \times T$), (2) The matrix multiplication $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top$ with arrows showing the operation, (3) The resulting Gram matrix $\mathbf{G}$ as a symmetric $C \times C$ heatmap. Use color coding to show how correlations between different feature channels are captured. Include annotations explaining that diagonal elements capture channel variances while off-diagonal elements capture co-activation patterns.]}
	\caption{Gram matrix computation captures second-order statistics. The matrix $\mathbf{G} = \frac{1}{T}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top$ encodes channel-to-channel correlations, where $\mathbf{G}_{ij}$ measures how features $i$ and $j$ co-activate across time.}
	\label{fig:gram_computation}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Theoretical Analysis of Texture Resonance Retrieval}
\label{sssec:trr_theory}

We provide theoretical justification for the effectiveness of TRR by analyzing its invariance properties and connection to parameter space.

\paragraph{Time-Invariance Property.}
Since TRR aggregates feature correlations across time through the Gram matrix $\mathbf{G} = \frac{1}{T}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top$, the representation is approximately invariant to temporal shifts.

\textbf{Proposition 1 (Approximate Time-Shift Invariance).} For any feature matrix $\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T}$ and its circularly time-shifted version $\mathbf{H}'$ (shifted by $\tau$ time steps), when $T \gg \tau$, we have:
\begin{equation}
	\|\mathbf{G} - \mathbf{G}'\|_F \approx 0
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{G} = \frac{1}{T}\mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top$ and $\mathbf{G}' = \frac{1}{T}\mathbf{H}'(\mathbf{H}')^\top$.

\textit{Intuition}: The Gram matrix aggregates correlations across all time pairs, making it insensitive to when patterns occur, only to what patterns co-occur. This is crucial for audio texture matching, where the same effect (e.g., chorus modulation) can start at different times but should be recognized as similar.

\paragraph{Scale-Invariance Property.}
The spectral normalization step ensures invariance to global energy scaling.

\textbf{Proposition 2 (Scale Invariance).} For any scaling factor $\alpha > 0$ and feature matrix $\mathbf{H}$, the normalized TRR embedding satisfies:
\begin{equation}
	\text{TRR}(\alpha \mathbf{H}) = \text{TRR}(\mathbf{H})
\end{equation}

\textit{Proof sketch}: For $\mathbf{H}' = \alpha \mathbf{H}$, we have $\mathbf{G}' = \frac{1}{T}(\alpha\mathbf{H})(\alpha\mathbf{H})^\top = \alpha^2 \mathbf{G}$. Since $\|\mathbf{G}'\|_F = \alpha^2 \|\mathbf{G}\|_F$, the normalized matrix is $\mathbf{G}'_{\text{norm}} = \frac{\alpha^2 \mathbf{G}}{\alpha^2 \|\mathbf{G}\|_F} = \frac{\mathbf{G}}{\|\mathbf{G}\|_F} = \mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}}$.

This property is essential for robustness to recording level variations and dynamic range differences between audio samples.

\paragraph{Connection to Parameter Space.}
Many audio effect families manifest primarily through changes in channel co-activation structure rather than mean feature magnitudes:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Saturation/Distortion}: Introduces correlated harmonics across frequency channels, creating strong off-diagonal correlations in $\mathbf{G}$
	\item \textbf{Chorus/Modulation}: Creates periodic temporal correlations that appear as structured patterns in the Gram matrix
	\item \textbf{Compression/Dynamics}: Alters variance relationships between channels, affecting diagonal and near-diagonal elements
\end{itemize}

Therefore, TRR acts as a \textit{texture prior} for retrieving parameter presets that activate appropriate effect subsets, rather than merely matching average spectral content.

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{figures/trr_vs_mean_pooling.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: TRR vs. Mean Pooling Comparison. This figure should show a side-by-side comparison for two audio samples: (1) A sustained distortion tone, (2) A modulated chorus tone. For each sample, show: (a) Waveform, (b) Mean-pooled embedding as a bar chart showing average channel activations, (c) TRR Gram matrix as a heatmap. Highlight how the two samples have similar mean-pooled embeddings (similar bar heights) but distinctly different Gram matrices (different correlation patterns). Use annotations to point out key differences in co-activation structure.]}
	\caption{Comparison of mean pooling vs. Texture Resonance Retrieval. While mean pooling captures first-order statistics (average channel activations), TRR captures second-order statistics (channel co-activation patterns), enabling discrimination of textures with similar average content but different structural properties.}
	\label{fig:trr_vs_mean}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Hybrid Retrieval Algorithm}

For a query $q = (T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}})$, we retrieve contextual examples from both knowledge bases:

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Dual-Modal Hybrid Retrieval with Uncertainty-Aware Fusion}
	\label{alg:dual_retrieval}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Query $q = (T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}})$, knowledge bases $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}, \mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$, top-K value $K$
		\ENSURE Fused top-K retrieval results $\mathcal{R}$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Text Retrieval}
		\STATE Encode text query: $\mathbf{e}_{\text{text}} = \text{SBERT}(T_{\text{desc}})$
		\STATE Retrieve top-$K$ candidates: $\mathcal{R}_{\text{text}} = \text{TopK}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}, \mathbf{e}_{\text{text}})$
		\STATE Compute text uncertainty: $u_{\text{text}} = \text{Entropy}(\text{Similarities}(\mathcal{R}_{\text{text}}))$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: Audio Retrieval (if reference available)}
		\IF{$A_{\text{ref}}$ is provided}
		\STATE Extract audio features: $\mathbf{h} = \text{Wav2Vec2}(A_{\text{ref}})$
		\STATE Compute TRR encoding: $\mathbf{G} = \text{TRR}(\mathbf{h})$
		\STATE Retrieve top-$K$ candidates: $\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}} = \text{TopK}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}, \mathbf{G})$
		\STATE Compute audio uncertainty: $u_{\text{audio}} = \text{Entropy}(\text{Similarities}(\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}))$
		\ELSE
		\STATE $\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}} = \emptyset$, $u_{\text{audio}} = \infty$
		\ENDIF

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Uncertainty-Aware Weight Computation}
		\STATE Compute modality weights:
		\STATE $w_{\text{text}} = \exp(-\beta \cdot u_{\text{text}})$
		\STATE $w_{\text{audio}} = \exp(-\beta \cdot u_{\text{audio}})$ \textit{where $\beta$ is temperature parameter}
		\STATE Normalize: $w_{\text{text}} \leftarrow \frac{w_{\text{text}}}{w_{\text{text}} + w_{\text{audio}}}$
		\STATE Normalize: $w_{\text{audio}} \leftarrow \frac{w_{\text{audio}}}{w_{\text{text}} + w_{\text{audio}}}$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 4: Fusion via Weighted Score Combination}
		\STATE Initialize fused candidates: $\mathcal{C} = \mathcal{R}_{\text{text}} \cup \mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}$
		\FORALL{candidate $c \in \mathcal{C}$}
		\STATE $s_{\text{text}}(c) = \text{Similarity}(c, T_{\text{desc}})$ if $c \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{text}}$ else 0
		\STATE $s_{\text{audio}}(c) = \text{Similarity}(c, A_{\text{ref}})$ if $c \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}$ else 0
		\STATE $s_{\text{fused}}(c) = w_{\text{text}} \cdot s_{\text{text}}(c) + w_{\text{audio}} \cdot s_{\text{audio}}(c)$
		\ENDFOR

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 5: Rank and Return Top-K}
		\STATE Sort $\mathcal{C}$ by $s_{\text{fused}}$ in descending order
		\STATE $\mathcal{R} \leftarrow$ Top-K candidates from sorted $\mathcal{C}$
		\RETURN $\mathcal{R}$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/dual_modal_retrieval.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Dual-Modal Retrieval Flow. This figure should show the complete dual-modal retrieval pipeline: (1) Left branch: Text description → SBERT encoding → Text retrieval from $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}$ → Top-K text results with uncertainty $u_{\text{text}}$; (2) Right branch: Audio reference → Wav2Vec2 + TRR encoding → Audio retrieval from $\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$ → Top-K audio results with uncertainty $u_{\text{audio}}$; (3) Center: Uncertainty-aware weight computation ($w_{\text{text}}, w_{\text{audio}}$); (4) Bottom: Fusion module combining weighted scores to produce final Top-K results. Use color coding for text (blue) and audio (orange) pathways, with arrows showing data flow.]}
	\caption{Dual-modal retrieval pipeline with uncertainty-aware fusion. The system retrieves candidates from both text and audio knowledge bases, estimates retrieval uncertainty for each modality, and fuses results using dynamic weights that down-weight unreliable modalities.}
	\label{fig:dual_modal_retrieval}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/modality_fusion.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Modality Fusion Mechanism. This figure should illustrate the fusion process: (1) Top: Two similarity distributions for text and audio retrieval results, visualized as histograms or bar charts; (2) Middle: Uncertainty computation showing how entropy is calculated from similarity distributions, with annotations showing high uncertainty (flat distribution) vs. low uncertainty (peaked distribution); (3) Bottom: Weight computation using exponential decay $w = \exp(-\beta \cdot u)$ with a curve showing how uncertainty maps to weight. Include a concrete example showing how vague text (high $u_{\text{text}}$, low $w_{\text{text}}$) gets down-weighted while confident audio (low $u_{\text{audio}}$, high $w_{\text{audio}}$) dominates the fusion.]}
	\caption{Uncertainty-aware modality fusion mechanism. Retrieval uncertainty is estimated from similarity distributions using entropy, then mapped to fusion weights via exponential decay. This allows the system to automatically down-weight unreliable modalities (e.g., vague text prompts) and rely more on confident modalities.}
	\label{fig:modality_fusion}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Theoretical Analysis of Dual-Modal Fusion}
\label{sssec:fusion_theory}

We provide theoretical justification for our uncertainty-aware fusion strategy.

\paragraph{Uncertainty Estimation via Entropy.}
For a retrieval modality $m \in \{\text{text}, \text{audio}\}$, let $S_m = \{s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_K\}$ be the similarity scores of the top-K retrieved candidates, normalized to sum to 1. We estimate retrieval uncertainty as:
\begin{equation}
	u_m = -\sum_{i=1}^K s_i \log s_i
\end{equation}

\textit{Intuition}: High entropy indicates a flat similarity distribution, suggesting the retrieval is uncertain (many equally plausible candidates). Low entropy indicates a peaked distribution, suggesting high confidence (one clear best match).

\paragraph{Adaptive Weight Computation.}
We map uncertainty to fusion weights using exponential decay:
\begin{equation}
	w_m = \frac{\exp(-\beta \cdot u_m)}{\sum_{m'} \exp(-\beta \cdot u_{m'})}
\end{equation}
where $\beta > 0$ controls the sensitivity to uncertainty.

\textbf{Proposition 3 (Monotonicity).} The fusion weight $w_m$ is monotonically decreasing in uncertainty $u_m$:
\begin{equation}
	\frac{\partial w_m}{\partial u_m} < 0
\end{equation}

\textit{Proof sketch}: Since $\exp(-\beta \cdot u_m)$ is monotonically decreasing in $u_m$, and the softmax normalization preserves monotonicity for two modalities, the result follows.

\paragraph{Robustness to Missing Modalities.}
When one modality is absent (e.g., $A_{\text{ref}} = \emptyset$), we set $u_{\text{audio}} = \infty$, which yields $w_{\text{audio}} = 0$ and $w_{\text{text}} = 1$, gracefully falling back to text-only retrieval.

\paragraph{Robustness to Degraded Inputs.}
When one modality provides low-quality input (e.g., vague text like ``make it sound good''), the retrieval produces a flat similarity distribution with high entropy $u_{\text{text}} \gg u_{\text{audio}}$. The fusion automatically down-weights the unreliable modality:
\begin{equation}
	w_{\text{text}} \approx 0, \quad w_{\text{audio}} \approx 1
\end{equation}
This explains the robustness results in Table \ref{tab:robustness}, where Audio-Agent remains stable under vague prompts by relying on audio-grounded retrieval.

\subsection{Adaptive Preference Learning}
\label{ssec:memory}

We view preference adaptation as a natural extension of the retrieval loop, but it is not exercised in the current experimental evaluation. The focus of this paper is on retrieval and reasoning under fixed knowledge bases, while personalized feedback-driven updates are left as future work.

\subsection{Parameter Space Constraints and Generation}
\label{ssec:param_constraints}

A critical challenge in LLM-based parameter generation is ensuring that outputs remain within physically valid and musically meaningful ranges. Unconstrained generation can produce parameters that violate plugin specifications (e.g., negative frequencies, ratios below 1:1) or are technically valid but perceptually incoherent (e.g., extreme settings that produce silence or clipping).

\subsubsection{Constraint Formalization}

We formalize the parameter space as $\Theta = \prod_{i=1}^d [\theta_i^{\min}, \theta_i^{\max}]$, where each parameter $\theta_i$ has a valid range $[\theta_i^{\min}, \theta_i^{\max}]$ defined by the plugin specification. Additionally, we enforce cross-parameter constraints $\mathcal{C}$ that capture dependencies between parameters (e.g., attack time must be less than release time in envelope-based effects).

\paragraph{Types of Constraints.}
\begin{enumerate}
	\item \textbf{Range Constraints}: $\theta_i \in [\theta_i^{\min}, \theta_i^{\max}]$ for all $i \in \{1, \ldots, d\}$
	\item \textbf{Dependency Constraints}: $\theta_i \leq \theta_j$ for specific parameter pairs (e.g., attack $\leq$ release)
	\item \textbf{Activation Constraints}: If effect $E_k$ is inactive, all its parameters must be at default values
	\item \textbf{Semantic Constraints}: Parameter combinations must be musically meaningful (e.g., compressor with ratio=1:1 and threshold=0dB is effectively bypassed)
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Parameter Generation with Constraint Satisfaction}

We employ a retrieval-guided generation strategy that combines LLM reasoning with explicit constraint checking and repair.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Constrained Parameter Generation}
	\label{alg:param_generation}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Query $q = (T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}})$, retrieved context $\mathcal{R}$, constraint set $\mathcal{C}$
		\ENSURE Valid parameter vector $\theta^* \in \Theta$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Build Contextual Prompt}
		\STATE Extract reference parameters: $\{\theta_1, \theta_2, \ldots, \theta_K\} \leftarrow \mathcal{R}$
		\STATE Format prompt: $P \leftarrow \text{FormatPrompt}(T_{\text{desc}}, \{\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_K\}, \mathcal{C})$
		\STATE \textit{Include constraint specifications and valid ranges in prompt}

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: LLM Generation with Structured Output}
		\STATE Generate candidate: $\theta_{\text{raw}} \leftarrow \text{LLM}(P)$
		\STATE Parse JSON output: $\theta_{\text{parsed}} \leftarrow \text{ParseJSON}(\theta_{\text{raw}})$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Constraint Validation}
		\STATE $\text{violations} \leftarrow \emptyset$
		\FORALL{parameter $\theta_i$ in $\theta_{\text{parsed}}$}
		\IF{$\theta_i < \theta_i^{\min}$ OR $\theta_i > \theta_i^{\max}$}
		\STATE Add $(i, \text{``range''})$ to violations
		\ENDIF
		\ENDFOR
		\FORALL{constraint $c \in \mathcal{C}$}
		\IF{$\neg c(\theta_{\text{parsed}})$}
		\STATE Add $(c, \text{``dependency''})$ to violations
		\ENDIF
		\ENDFOR

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 4: Constraint Repair}
		\IF{violations $\neq \emptyset$}
		\STATE $\theta^* \leftarrow \text{RepairConstraints}(\theta_{\text{parsed}}, \text{violations}, \mathcal{R})$
		\ELSE
		\STATE $\theta^* \leftarrow \theta_{\text{parsed}}$
		\ENDIF

		\RETURN $\theta^*$

		\STATE
		\STATE \textbf{Complexity Analysis:}
		\STATE \textit{Time}: $\mathcal{O}(d)$ for range checks, $\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{C}|)$ for dependency checks
		\STATE \textit{Space}: $\mathcal{O}(d)$ for parameter storage
		\STATE \textit{LLM inference cost dominates, but is amortized across queries}
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Constraint Repair via Nearest Valid Projection}
	\label{alg:constraint_repair}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Invalid parameter $\theta_{\text{invalid}}$, violations, retrieved references $\mathcal{R}$
		\ENSURE Repaired parameter $\theta_{\text{repaired}} \in \Theta$

		\STATE Initialize: $\theta_{\text{repaired}} \leftarrow \theta_{\text{invalid}}$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Fix Range Violations via Clipping}
		\FORALL{$(i, \text{``range''}) \in$ violations}
		\STATE $\theta_{\text{repaired}}[i] \leftarrow \text{clip}(\theta_{\text{invalid}}[i], \theta_i^{\min}, \theta_i^{\max})$
		\ENDFOR

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: Fix Dependency Violations}
		\FORALL{$(c, \text{``dependency''}) \in$ violations}
		\STATE Find nearest reference satisfying $c$: $\theta_{\text{ref}} \leftarrow \arg\min_{\theta \in \mathcal{R}, c(\theta)} \|\theta - \theta_{\text{repaired}}\|_2$
		\STATE Interpolate: $\theta_{\text{repaired}} \leftarrow 0.7 \cdot \theta_{\text{repaired}} + 0.3 \cdot \theta_{\text{ref}}$
		\STATE \textit{Weighted blend favors generated values while ensuring constraint satisfaction}
		\ENDFOR

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Verify All Constraints}
		\IF{$\exists c \in \mathcal{C}: \neg c(\theta_{\text{repaired}})$}
		\STATE \textit{Fallback: Use nearest retrieved reference}
		\STATE $\theta_{\text{repaired}} \leftarrow \arg\min_{\theta \in \mathcal{R}} \|\theta - \theta_{\text{invalid}}\|_2$
		\ENDIF

		\RETURN $\theta_{\text{repaired}}$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/constraint_satisfaction.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Constraint Satisfaction Mechanism. This figure should illustrate the constraint checking and repair process: (1) Top: LLM generates raw parameters shown as a parameter vector with some values highlighted in red (violations); (2) Middle: Constraint validation showing range checks (min/max bounds) and dependency checks (e.g., attack < release) with arrows pointing to violations; (3) Bottom: Repair process showing clipping for range violations and interpolation with retrieved references for dependency violations. Use color coding: green for valid parameters, red for violations, yellow for repaired parameters. Include a flowchart showing the decision logic: if violations exist → repair → re-validate → fallback if needed.]}
	\caption{Constraint satisfaction mechanism. The system validates generated parameters against range and dependency constraints, repairs violations through clipping and reference-guided interpolation, and falls back to retrieved references if repair fails.}
	\label{fig:constraint_satisfaction}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	%\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/parameter_space_mapping.pdf}
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Parameter Space Mapping. This figure should visualize the mapping from retrieval results to parameter space: (1) Left: Retrieved presets shown as points in a 2D projection of parameter space (use t-SNE or PCA), color-coded by style/genre; (2) Center: Query point (text + audio) shown as a star, with arrows pointing to top-K retrieved neighbors; (3) Right: Generated parameter shown as a new point, with a convex hull or shaded region indicating the valid parameter space $\Theta$. Show constraint boundaries as dashed lines. Include annotations explaining how retrieval constrains the generation to a feasible neighborhood, and how constraint repair projects invalid outputs back into $\Theta$.]}
	\caption{Parameter space mapping from retrieval to generation. Retrieved presets define a neighborhood in parameter space that guides LLM generation. Constraint satisfaction ensures the output remains within the valid parameter space $\Theta$ while preserving proximity to retrieved references.}
	\label{fig:param_space_mapping}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Theoretical Guarantees}

\paragraph{Constraint Satisfaction Guarantee.}
\textbf{Theorem 1 (Constraint Satisfaction).} Algorithm \ref{alg:constraint_repair} always produces a parameter vector $\theta_{\text{repaired}} \in \Theta$ that satisfies all constraints in $\mathcal{C}$.

\textit{Proof sketch}: Range clipping ensures $\theta_i \in [\theta_i^{\min}, \theta_i^{\max}]$ for all $i$. For dependency constraints, if interpolation fails, the fallback to the nearest retrieved reference guarantees constraint satisfaction since all references in $\mathcal{R}$ are pre-validated. $\square$

\paragraph{Proximity Preservation.}
While constraint repair modifies the generated parameters, it aims to preserve proximity to the original LLM output. We quantify this via the repair distance:
\begin{equation}
	\Delta_{\text{repair}} = \|\theta_{\text{repaired}} - \theta_{\text{invalid}}\|_2
\end{equation}

In our experiments (Table \ref{tab:hallucination}), the average repair distance is small (typically < 5\% of parameter range), indicating that most LLM outputs are nearly valid and require only minor corrections.

\subsection{Complete Inference Pipeline}

The full parameter inference process combines retrieval, reasoning, and constraint satisfaction:

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Audio-Agent Parameter Inference}
	\label{alg:inference}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Query $q = (T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}})$, history $\mathcal{H}$, knowledge base $\mathcal{K}$
		\ENSURE Parameter vector $\theta^*$

		\STATE Preprocess query: $q_{\text{proc}} = \text{Preprocess}(q)$
		\STATE Retrieve context: $\mathcal{R} = \text{DualModalRetrieval}(q_{\text{proc}}, \mathcal{K})$
		\STATE Build context: $\text{Context} = \text{FormatContext}(\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{H})$
		\STATE Generate parameters: $\theta^* = \text{LLM}(q_{\text{proc}}, \text{Context})$
		\STATE Validate output: $\theta^* \leftarrow \text{Validate}(\theta^*)$
		\RETURN $\theta^*$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\textbf{Complexity Analysis}: The inference cost scales with knowledge base size, embedding dimensionality, and generation length. In our experimental setting, retrieval and generation are lightweight, and we focus on comparative performance rather than tight runtime guarantees.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We construct an evaluation set of 50 guitar tone samples by merging the parameter database with the corresponding audio-vector database. Each item includes a style description, parameter preset, and audio vector used for retrieval and evaluation.

