\textbf{Baselines}: (1) \textit{Zero-shot}: direct parameter generation without retrieval. (2) \textit{Text-RAG}: retrieval using text descriptions only. (3) \textit{Vector-RAG}: retrieval using audio vectors only. (4) \textit{Audio-Agent}: our texture-aware dual retrieval with candidate fusion.

\subsection{RQ1: Overall Effectiveness}

We evaluate Audio-Agent against three baseline methods across comprehensive objective metrics. Table \ref{tab:main_results} presents the primary evaluation results on our 50-style guitar dataset.

\begin{table}[t]
	\centering
	\caption{Main Results on 50 Guitar Tone Samples}
	\label{tab:main_results}
	\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Method}             & \textbf{Param. Dist. (L2)} & \textbf{Acc@0.1} & \textbf{Cos}  & \textbf{Active Recall} \\
		\midrule
		Zero-shot                   & 42.67                      & 0.12             & 0.45          & 0.15                   \\
		Text-RAG                    & 41.37                      & 0.15             & 0.48          & 0.18                   \\
		Vector-RAG                  & 22.27                      & 0.38             & 0.72          & 0.41                   \\
		\midrule
		\textbf{Audio-Agent (Ours)} & \textbf{21.86}             & \textbf{0.45}    & \textbf{0.78} & \textbf{0.49}          \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\csname textbf\endcsname{What.} Table \ref{tab:main_results} shows that Audio-Agent substantially reduces parameter error compared to the zero-shot baseline and improves tolerance accuracy, direction consistency, and active-parameter recall.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Why.} Retrieval reduces the difficulty from inventing parameters to adapting known-good exemplars. The strong gap between Text-RAG and Vector-RAG indicates that audio evidence provides tighter physical constraints than ambiguous text.

\csname textbf\endcsname{So What.} The large gain in \textit{Active Recall} suggests that the system is better at identifying which effects should be engaged (a critical property for sparse parameter vectors).

\subsubsection{Why Does Text-Only Perform So Poorly?}

Text-RAG's marginal improvement over zero-shot reflects semantic ambiguity in production descriptors. For example, a query like \textit{``warm vintage breakup''} can retrieve stylistically plausible but functionally mismatched references such as ``Warm Jazz Clean'' (warm but insufficient gain), ``Vintage Rock'' (vague and under-specified), or ``British Crunch'' (correct family but wrong breakup point). Without audio grounding, text retrieval cannot disambiguate which interpretation matches the intended texture.

\subsubsection{Where Does TRR Help?}

TRR provides the most benefit when the target sound is defined by interaction patterns (e.g., breakup dynamics, modulation, or subtle presence shaping) rather than by average spectral content. In these cases, mean-pooled embeddings can retrieve broadly similar tones while missing discriminative temporal co-activation structure. We analyze such texture-sensitive long-tail behavior in RQ2 via the ``Stadium Rock'' case study (Table \ref{tab:exp4}).

\subsection{Metrics}

We evaluate on four metrics aligned with our implementation: (1) \textit{Parameter Distance} (RMSE over flattened parameter vectors), (2) \textit{Acc@0.1} (fraction of parameters within a tolerance of 0.1), (3) \textit{Cosine Similarity} (direction consistency between parameter vectors), and (4) \textit{Active Recall} (recall over non-zero parameters).

\begin{figure}[h]
	\centering
	\textcolor{red}{[Figure: Metric Computation Visualization. This figure should show the computation process for each metric: (1) Top-left: Parameter Distance - show two parameter vectors $\theta_{\text{pred}}$ and $\theta_{\text{gt}}$, with L2 distance formula and geometric interpretation; (2) Top-right: Acc@0.1 - show parameter-wise absolute differences, with highlighting of parameters within tolerance ($< 0.1$) vs. outside tolerance; (3) Bottom-left: Cosine Similarity - show parameter vectors in high-dimensional space, with angle computation formula; (4) Bottom-right: Active Recall - show confusion matrix comparing active (non-zero) parameters between predicted and ground truth, with recall computation. Use color coding: green for correct, red for incorrect.]}
	\caption{Evaluation metrics computation. The figure illustrates how each metric is computed from predicted and ground-truth parameter vectors.}
	\label{fig:metric_computation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{RQ2: Texture Validity and Mechanism}

To validate the effectiveness of our proposed Texture Resonance Retrieval (TRR) against traditional Vector Similarity, we conduct comparative experiments using synthetic audio data. Table \ref{tab:exp4} presents the retrieval error (L2 distance) for two distinct musical styles.

\begin{table}[h]
	\centering
	\caption{Case Study: Texture Resonance vs. Vector Baseline (Lower is Better)}
	\label{tab:exp4}
	\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Style} & \textbf{Test Sample} & \textbf{Vector-RAG} & \textbf{Texture-RAG (Ours)} \\ \midrule
		Clean          & Stadium Rock         & 18.51               & \textbf{8.01}               \\
		Distortion     & Punk Rock Raw        & 53.31               & \textbf{51.14}              \\
		Vintage        & Tweed Breakup        & 10.72               & \textbf{7.74}               \\ \bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\csname textbf\endcsname{What.} Table \ref{tab:exp4} highlights texture-sensitive cases where TRR improves parameter alignment, especially for subtle overdrive dynamics and clean textures where mean pooling can over-smooth discriminative cues.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Why.} Mean pooling emphasizes average content and can conflate signals that share similar global statistics. TRR instead captures channel co-activation structure that is sensitive to non-linear harmonic coupling and modulation.

\csname textbf\endcsname{So What.} These results support the Texture Hypothesis: correlation-based descriptors provide a better inductive bias for texture-grounded retrieval in long-tail timbre design.

\paragraph{Interpretation.} TRR is not a universal substitute for vector similarity; instead, it complements it by favoring exemplars that preserve co-activation structure. This is particularly useful in neighborhoods where multiple presets share similar mean content but differ in second-order interaction patterns.

\subsubsection{Case Study: ``Stadium Rock'' Retrieval Granularity}

We analyze the ``Stadium Rock'' sample (Table \ref{tab:exp4}) to illustrate a typical retrieval granularity failure mode for vector embeddings.

\paragraph{Ground truth.} ``Stadium Rock'' is characterized by a bright, clean tone with pronounced high-mid presence that needs to be expressed via subtle EQ and dynamics choices, rather than by changing the overall effect family.

\paragraph{Retrieval.} In Vector-RAG, the nearest neighbor can be dominated by broad spectral similarity (e.g., ``clean guitar''), retrieving an example such as ``Funk Clean'' that matches overall brightness but misses the specific ``stadium'' presence profile. In contrast, TRR retrieval is more sensitive to co-activation patterns in upper-mid frequency channels, yielding a closer reference neighborhood.

\paragraph{Prediction.} When the retrieved context is too coarse, the generator tends to copy a plausible clean preset but fails to recover the small, perceptually salient presence shaping needed for the target. With TRR-grounded context, the inferred parameters better preserve the interaction pattern associated with the target texture, reducing the downstream parameter error in this case.

\subsubsection{Feature--Parameter Alignment}

To quantify how well feature similarity predicts parameter similarity, we compute pairwise correlations between (i) feature-space similarity and (ii) cosine similarity between ground-truth parameter vectors across all sample pairs. We report Spearman's $\rho$ for all pairs and a subset focusing on distortion/modulation-heavy (non-stationary) tones.

\begin{table}[t]
	\centering
	\caption{Feature--Parameter Alignment (Spearman $\rho$; higher is better)}
	\label{tab:feature_param_corr}
	\small
	\begin{tabular}{lcc}
		\csname toprule\endcsname
		\csname textbf\endcsname{Feature Space} & \csname textbf\endcsname{All Pairs} & \csname textbf\endcsname{Non-Stationary Pairs} \\
		\midrule
		Mean Vector (Vector-RAG)                & 0.205                               & 0.341                                          \\
		Texture Resonance (TRR)                 & -0.005                              & 0.036                                          \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We observe that global feature--parameter alignment is weak for TRR, consistent with the many-to-one nature of timbre: multiple parameter configurations can yield perceptually similar textures. This motivates our framing of TRR as a \textit{retrieval prior} that helps on texture-sensitive long-tail cases (Table \ref{tab:exp4}) rather than as a globally calibrated proxy for parameter similarity.

Across qualitative inspection, we observe three recurring failure modes. First, tones involving effect types not well covered by the preset database can lead to retrieval mismatch and over-regularized generation, as the system defaults to the nearest available exemplar rather than extrapolating to unseen combinations. Second, text descriptors can bias retrieval toward a genre-consistent but texture-inconsistent neighborhood; for instance, queries containing ``vintage'' may pull clean references when breakup is actually required, conflating stylistic era with technical effect type. Third, small but perceptually salient deviations, such as a presence boost around 2--3\,kHz, can be hard to recover without higher-resolution conditioning or explicit EQ-band priors, as these subtle adjustments may not manifest strongly in global texture embeddings.

\subsection{Ablation Study}

To isolate the contribution of each component, we compare Text-RAG, Vector-RAG, and Texture-RAG configurations under the same evaluation metrics.

\textbf{Analysis}: We isolate component contributions by comparing Text-RAG, Vector-RAG, and Texture-RAG (Ours). The improvements from Vector-RAG to Texture-RAG indicate the benefit of texture-aware retrieval, while the gap between Text-RAG and Vector-RAG highlights the importance of audio evidence in parameter inference.

\subsection{Perceptual Listening Test (Planned)}

We have prepared a MUSHRA-style evaluation interface for subjective assessment, but a full listening study and statistical analysis are left to future work. In the current submission, we focus on objective parameter-based evaluation.

\subsection{RQ3: Robustness Under Degraded Inputs}

We evaluate robustness under a text-degradation scenario that mirrors ambiguous user prompts. We replace the prompt with a vague instruction (e.g., ``Make it sound good with some effects.'') and compare Text-RAG with our texture-aware retrieval.

\begin{table}[t]
	\centering
	\caption{Robustness Under Vague Text}
	\label{tab:robustness}
	\begin{tabular}{lcc}
		\toprule
		\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Text-RAG} & \textbf{Audio-Agent (Ours)} \\
		\midrule
		Vague Text         & 45.35             & \textbf{26.17}              \\
		\bottomrule
	\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\csname textbf\endcsname{What.} Under vague prompts, text-only retrieval collapses, while Audio-Agent remains substantially more stable.

\csname textbf\endcsname{Why.} The fusion policy can down-weight low-information text and rely on audio-grounded retrieval.

\csname textbf\endcsname{So What.} This behavior is essential for real-world usage, where user prompts are often under-specified and noisy.

\paragraph{Interpretation.} These results suggest that modality reliability should be treated as a first-class signal: when text collapses to generic intent, audio-conditioned retrieval can still constrain feasible parameter neighborhoods and stabilize generation.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Broader Implications}

Audio-Agent suggests a practical pattern for grounded creative assistants: treat the LLM as a reasoning layer, while using retrieval over real presets to provide feasibility constraints and stylistic priors. This reduces hallucination and keeps outputs editable within standard production workflows. More broadly, it shifts the emphasis from open-ended generation to data-efficient adaptation, improving sample efficiency (reuse of real presets), interpretability (traceable references), and controllability (parameter-level editability).

\subsection{Revisiting the Texture Hypothesis}

We observe that texture-aware retrieval helps on specific long-tail cases (Table \ref{tab:exp4}) and remains robust under vague prompts (Table \ref{tab:robustness}). However, Table \ref{tab:feature_param_corr} shows that TRR similarity is not a globally calibrated proxy for parameter similarity. This motivates a careful interpretation: TRR provides a \textit{retrieval prior} that can be beneficial in texture-sensitive neighborhoods, rather than a universal metric for parameter transfer.

An open direction is whether analogous second-order representations help bridge modality gaps in other continuous control settings (e.g., video textures, haptic signals, or physiological time series), where the ``style'' of a signal may be expressed through co-activation structure rather than pointwise magnitudes. Future work can validate this hypothesis via controlled ablations on non-stationarity and by pairing retrieval with human preference judgments.

Our approach has several limitations. First, retrieval quality fundamentally depends on database diversity; out-of-distribution tones that are not well-represented in the preset corpus remain challenging to handle. Second, while richer databases improve coverage, they also increase retrieval cost and require careful indexing and caching strategies to maintain acceptable query latencies. Third, the parameter identifiability problem persists: multiple parameter configurations can yield perceptually similar textures, making objective parameter distance an imperfect surrogate for perceptual match. Fourth, the subjectivity of tone quality means that what constitutes a ``good'' sound depends heavily on musical context and user intent, which objective metrics cannot fully capture. Fifth, the editability--synthesis trade-off is fundamental: constraining outputs to editable presets improves usability and workflow integration but may limit the reachable sound space compared to direct waveform synthesis approaches. Finally, our evaluation scope focuses on objective metrics derived from parameter alignment; controlled listening tests remain necessary to measure perceptual preference and validate whether the generated parameters align with human judgment of sonic quality.

\subsection{Ethical Considerations}

Audio-Agent is intended to augment user creativity rather than replace it. Risks include homogenization toward common exemplar neighborhoods and over-reliance on suggested settings. In deployment, the interface should emphasize transparency (e.g., showing retrieved references) and user agency (e.g., direct editability and opt-out of retrieval). More generally, any model or retrieval corpus built from existing recordings or presets should consider copyright, licensing, and data provenance constraints.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented Audio-Agent, a neuro-symbolic system for generating editable DSP parameters from natural language descriptions with dual-modal, texture-aware retrieval. Across objective metrics (Table \ref{tab:main_results}) and robustness settings (Table \ref{tab:robustness}), retrieval grounding improves parameter alignment compared to text-only or zero-shot baselines.

\subsection{Immediate Next Steps}

In the short term, the most actionable next steps are (i) expanding preset coverage for under-represented effect families, (ii) improving transparency by exposing reference audio and parameter deltas, and (iii) validating retrieval quality with controlled listening tests.

\subsection{Roadmap}

Looking forward, we envision a three-stage development path. In the \textbf{short term}, we plan to expand preset coverage for under-represented effect families and improve retrieval transparency by surfacing reference audio and parameter deltas to the user, enabling better understanding of how the system arrives at its recommendations. In the \textbf{medium term}, we aim to integrate controlled listening tests to validate perceptual quality and develop richer conditioning mechanisms that better capture time-varying structure in audio processing chains. Over the \textbf{long term}, we seek to generalize beyond guitar effects to broader production parameter spaces, including mixing, mastering, and synthesis workflows, thereby enabling grounded, editable control across diverse creative applications.

We hope this approach lowers the barrier to expressive sound design and broadens creative access to professional-grade production control.

\subsubsection*{Acknowledgments}
We thank the anonymous reviewers for their valuable feedback.

\appendix

\section{Additional Algorithm Details}

\subsection{Complete TRR Algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Complete Texture Resonance Retrieval Algorithm}
	\label{alg:trr_complete_appendix}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Audio sample $a$, sample rate $sr=16000$, Wav2Vec2 model $\mathcal{W}$
		\ENSURE TRR embedding $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}} \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times C}$ where $C=768$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Audio Preprocessing}
		\STATE Resample $a$ to 16kHz if needed
		\STATE Normalize amplitude: $a \leftarrow a / \max(|a|)$
		\STATE Ensure minimum length: $T_{\text{audio}} \geq 1$ second

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: Feature Extraction}
		\STATE Extract frame-level features from layer 9 of Wav2Vec2:
		\STATE $\mathbf{H} \leftarrow \mathcal{W}_{\text{layer 9}}(a) \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times T}$
		\STATE \textit{where $C=768$ is feature dimension, $T$ is number of time frames}

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Gram Matrix Computation}
		\STATE Compute second-order statistics:
		\STATE $\mathbf{G} \leftarrow \frac{1}{T} \mathbf{H}\mathbf{H}^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times C}$
		\STATE \textit{$\mathbf{G}_{ij}$ captures correlation between channels $i$ and $j$}

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 4: Spectral Normalization}
		\STATE Compute Frobenius norm: $\sigma \leftarrow \|\mathbf{G}\|_F = \sqrt{\sum_{i,j} \mathbf{G}_{ij}^2}$
		\STATE Normalize for scale invariance: $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}} \leftarrow \mathbf{G} / \sigma$

		\RETURN $\mathbf{G}_{\text{norm}}$

		\STATE
		\STATE \textbf{Complexity Analysis:}
		\STATE \textit{Time}: $\mathcal{O}(T \cdot C)$ for feature extraction, $\mathcal{O}(C^2 \cdot T)$ for Gram matrix
		\STATE \textit{Space}: $\mathcal{O}(C \cdot T)$ for features, $\mathcal{O}(C^2)$ for Gram matrix
		\STATE \textit{Total}: $\mathcal{O}(C^2 \cdot T)$ time, $\mathcal{O}(C^2)$ space when features are not cached
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Complete Dual-Modal Retrieval Algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[t]
	\caption{Complete Dual-Modal Retrieval with Uncertainty-Aware Fusion}
	\label{alg:dual_retrieval_appendix}
	\begin{algorithmic}
		\REQUIRE Query $q = (T_{\text{desc}}, A_{\text{ref}})$, knowledge bases $\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}, \mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}$, top-K value $K$
		\ENSURE Fused top-K retrieval results $\mathcal{R}$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 1: Text Retrieval}
		\STATE Encode text query: $\mathbf{e}_{\text{text}} = \text{SBERT}(T_{\text{desc}})$
		\STATE Retrieve top-$K$ candidates: $\mathcal{R}_{\text{text}} = \text{TopK}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{text}}, \mathbf{e}_{\text{text}})$
		\STATE Compute text uncertainty: $u_{\text{text}} = \text{Entropy}(\text{Similarities}(\mathcal{R}_{\text{text}}))$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 2: Audio Retrieval (if reference available)}
		\IF{$A_{\text{ref}}$ is provided}
		\STATE Extract audio features: $\mathbf{h} = \text{Wav2Vec2}(A_{\text{ref}})$
		\STATE Compute TRR encoding: $\mathbf{G} = \text{TRR}(\mathbf{h})$
		\STATE Retrieve top-$K$ candidates: $\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}} = \text{TopK}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{audio}}, \mathbf{G})$
		\STATE Compute audio uncertainty: $u_{\text{audio}} = \text{Entropy}(\text{Similarities}(\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}))$
		\ELSE
		\STATE $\mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}} = \emptyset$, $u_{\text{audio}} = \infty$
		\ENDIF

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 3: Uncertainty-Aware Weight Computation}
		\STATE Compute modality weights:
		\STATE $w_{\text{text}} = \exp(-\beta \cdot u_{\text{text}})$
		\STATE $w_{\text{audio}} = \exp(-\beta \cdot u_{\text{audio}})$ \textit{where $\beta$ is temperature parameter}
		\STATE Normalize: $w_{\text{text}} \leftarrow \frac{w_{\text{text}}}{w_{\text{text}} + w_{\text{audio}}}$
		\STATE Normalize: $w_{\text{audio}} \leftarrow \frac{w_{\text{audio}}}{w_{\text{text}} + w_{\text{audio}}}$

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 4: Fusion via Weighted Score Combination}
		\STATE Initialize fused candidates: $\mathcal{C} = \mathcal{R}_{\text{text}} \cup \mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}$
		\FORALL{candidate $c \in \mathcal{C}$}
		\STATE $s_{\text{text}}(c) = \text{Similarity}(c, T_{\text{desc}})$ if $c \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{text}}$ else 0
		\STATE $s_{\text{audio}}(c) = \text{Similarity}(c, A_{\text{ref}})$ if $c \in \mathcal{R}_{\text{audio}}$ else 0
		\STATE $s_{\text{fused}}(c) = w_{\text{text}} \cdot s_{\text{text}}(c) + w_{\text{audio}} \cdot s_{\text{audio}}(c)$
		\ENDFOR

		\STATE \textbf{// Step 5: Rank and Return Top-K}
		\STATE Sort $\mathcal{C}$ by $s_{\text{fused}}$ in descending order
		\STATE $\mathcal{R} \leftarrow$ Top-K candidates from sorted $\mathcal{C}$
		\RETURN $\mathcal{R}$
	\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

